WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.850
Data centres burn electricity and evaporate water to cool servers.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.850
Many jobs could run at another site, or later.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.850
GreenQ picks a smarter place and time with quantum computing.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.850
Four jobs, two data centres, day and night slots.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.850
Run now: every job starts at home;
Site A is crowded at night.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.850
Run smart: jobs move to cleaner slots and drier cooling.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.850
530 kilograms less CO2 and 1,798 litres less water.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.850
Dewy explains each move.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.850
Carbon and water pull against each other.
Watch the slider.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.350
Carbon only saves 593 kilograms; water only saves 2,134 litres.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.850
The three IBM marks ran on a real quantum computer.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.350
The real circuit: 8 qubits, a where and a when per job.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.850
Every answer is a valid schedule;
the usual method needs 16.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.850
It ran on IBM Marrakesh: 114 qubit links, 7 runs.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.850
Random guessing puts 10 percent in the best tenth;
IBM put 18 to 23.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.850
Noise-reduction tricks did not help consistently.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.350
We ask GreenQ.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.850
Answers come only from saved, fact-checked results.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.350
Then the honest question.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.850
No, not at this size. A laptop is faster;
it is our referee.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.850
Simple methods also find the best plan here, and faster.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.850
Bigger problems need many more qubit links.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.350
530 kilograms less CO2, 1,798 litres less water.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.850
No quantum advantage claimed. Next: bigger problems and real data.
